  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 358 AM EDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\n- Quiet weather regime continues through Tuesday\n\nDry weather continues through Tuesday as ridging hangs on through\nthat time. It is not until during the day on Tuesday that the\nsurface high finally begins to slip away to the east in response \nto a system moving across Canada. It is this system that will push\nthe east coast low out to sea. High confidence in dry weather \nthrough Tuesday and maybe even Tuesday night as the new GFS and \nECMWF ensembles that just came in show all 80 members dry at least\nthrough Tuesday evening. \n\nMain focus in the this period is to watch for the threat of\nmorning fog and stratus in a moist flow off of Lake Huron. Just\nbeginning to see some stratus now towards Saginaw Bay in the\nnighttime microphysics imagery. Sites along U.S. 127 are\ndefinitely showing fog and we will be monitoring visibilities. A\nsimilar setup presents itself tonight into Monday morning.\n\n- Rain chances Tuesday night through Thursday night \n\nNot a significant amount of change in the models during the mid\nweek time frame. We are still expecting rain associated with\nmoisture transport on Wednesday. A cold front is forecast to \npress in from the northwest on Thursday. The surge in moisture \nfrom the southwest with tropical origins still tries to press in, \nbut the models look less impressive tonight. 7 day totals of \nrainfall may push to around an inch in our south and especially \nsouthwest, but this is not overly concerning.\n\n- Fair weather again for Friday and Saturday\n\nHigh pressure quickly returns for Friday and Saturday with dry\nweather returning after a couple days of rain. In terms of\ntemperatures through the entire 7 day period we will be above\nnormal much of the time, but cooling off into Friday and Saturday\nas northwest flow advects in colder air. Highs back into the 60s\nafter many days in the 70s.",
